    "body": "Copper cathodes being loaded onto a ship. Credit: Atlantic Copper.\nBy: Northern Miner Staff November 20, 2026\nTraders have booked an estimated 30,000 to 100,000 tonnes of refined copper to ship from the West Coast of the United States to China, reversing a trade that filled American warehouses ahead of a possible U.S. import tariff, BMO Capital Markets said Friday.\nChina’s Yangshan warrant premium , a benchmark for imported copper held in bonded warehouses, reached a record $500 (C$715.9) per tonne above London prices Thursday. Rising Chinese premiums and a narrowing gap between U.S. and London futures prices have made China a more profitable destination for some traders, BMO analysts Helen Amos and George Heppel wrote from Asia Copper Week in Shanghai.\n“Copper units are now starting to leave the U.S. west coast destined for China, an event we had assigned a very low probability to,” Amos and Heppel noted.\nBMO identifies the booked cargoes as copper held outside exchange warehouses on the West Coast. It estimates a voyage of about two weeks before the metal reaches China.\nTariff split\nThe split in the copper market runs deeper than the cost of shipping. Traders BMO spoke to in China generally expect U.S. President Donald Trump to abandon the proposed refined-copper duty. Western market participants still lean towards a tariff, the analysts said.\nA July 2025 proclamation by the White House imposed tariffs on semi-finished copper products and derivatives. It also called for further review before a decision on refined-metal duties of 15% from Jan. 1, 2027, and 30% from Jan. 1, 2028.\nThree-month copper on the London Metal Exchange traded at $12,310.50 per tonne at 5:30 a.m. Toronto time Friday, about 8.2% lower on the week, according to Reuters . The metal had already fallen below $13,000 on Wednesday. BMO linked the sell-off to investors retreating from artificial-intelligence trades, even as physical supplies in China remained tight.\nState-owned Chilean producer Codelco, meanwhile, has offered Asian buyers annual contract premiums of $250 to $280 per tonne for 2027, compared with $350 for Chinese buyers this year, Reuters reported, citing people familiar with the discussions. China imported just over 140,000 tonnes of Chilean cathode in the first nine months of 2026, down 38% from a year earlier, the report added.\nA signal from Washington could arrest the slide. Otherwise, copper may have to fall far enough to curb scrap supply or higher-cost production from leaching, potentially to around $10,000 to $11,000 per tonne, Amos and Heppel said. BMO’s medium-term bullish view on copper is unchanged.\nPrint\nTAG: copper copper production critical minerals\nCOMMODITY: Copper\nREGION: Africa Asia Australia, NZ & South Pacific Canada Europe South America United States\nRepublish this article",
